data.content:
# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Critical Issues in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Works but is [obsolete and discouraged](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.threading.thread.interrupt) |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | Hand-rolled producer/consumer — replaced by `Channel<T>` |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` drops items |
| No cancellation | No cooperative cancellation model |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _processingTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        // ReadAllAsync yields items as they arrive; completes when
        // the writer is marked complete and the channel drains.
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        // TryWrite on an unbounded channel always succeeds
        // unless the writer has been completed.
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Processor has been stopped.");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        // 1. Signal no more items → channel reader completes naturally
        _channel.Writer.Complete();

        if (_processingTask is not null)
        {
            // 2. Give the loop time to drain remaining items
            using var timeout = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _processingTask.WaitAsync(timeout.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Drain timed out — cancel the loop
                await _cts.CancelAsync();
            }
        }

        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // your processing logic
    }
}
```

## Key Migration Decisions

### 1. `Channel<T>` replaces `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock`
The original had a **race condition**: two rapid `Enqueue` calls could signal once, processing only one item. `Channel<T>` is a lock-free, thread-safe producer/consumer primitive that handles all of this correctly.

### 2. Cooperative cancellation replaces `Abort`/`Interrupt`
```
Thread.Abort()   →  CancellationTokenSource.CancelAsync()
Thread.Interrupt() →  (not needed; channel completion ends the loop)
```
`CancellationToken` flows through `ReadAllAsync`, causing it to throw `OperationCanceledException` when cancelled.

### 3. `IAsyncDisposable` replaces `Stop()`
Allows `await using` at call sites:
```csharp
await using var processor = new EventProcessor();
processor.Start();
processor.Enqueue("hello");
// automatically drains and shuts down at end of scope
```

### 4. Graceful → forced shutdown (two-phase)
The original `Join(5000)` then `Abort()` maps to:
- **Phase 1**: `Writer.Complete()` — lets the loop drain remaining items
- **Phase 2**: `CancelAsync()` after 5s timeout — forces the loop to exit

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---|---|
| Using `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` "because it still compiles" | It works but misses perf/correctness wins. `Channel<T>` is allocation-free on the hot path. |
| Catching `ThreadAbortException` in migrated code | It's never thrown in .NET 8 — dead code. Remove it. |
| Forgetting to call `Writer.Complete()` | `ReadAllAsync` will **hang forever** waiting for more items. |
| Blocking inside `Handle()` with `Task.Result` | Blocks the processing task. Use `async` handler if it does I/O. |
| Using bounded channels without backpressure handling | `TryWrite` returns `false` when full — you must handle it or use `WriteAsync`. |
